{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The lotteries involve monetary losses with low probabilities. The outcomes are -6000 (Lottery B) and -3000 (Lottery A), both with unfavorable outcomes (losses), and the probabilities of these losses are 0.1% and 0.2%, respectively. The probability of a 'favorable outcome' (i.e., 0 Euros, no loss) is 99.9% for Lottery B and 99.8% for Lottery A. Thus, Lottery B has a slightly higher probability (by 0.1 percentage points) of the favorable outcome (0 Euros) compared to Lottery A. According to the decision reason, since the outcome differences are considered negligible, the preference should go to the lottery with the higher probability of the favorable outcome — in this case, Lottery B.",
  "step_2": "The individual considers the psychological impact of losses, specifically referencing 'Schmerzaversion' (pain aversion), and compares it to the case with positive outcomes. They note that the probability difference is 'verschwindend gering' (vanishingly small), but the outcome difference is 'umso gravierender' (all the more significant). The individual explicitly shifts focus from probability to the magnitude of the loss, stating that when comparing the worst cases, they would 'much rather' lose 3000 than 6000 Euros. This indicates that the individual prioritized the size of the potential loss over the probability of the favorable outcome. The justification centers on minimizing the maximum possible loss, not on maximizing the chance of avoiding any loss.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason emphasizes the probability of the favorable outcome (0 Euros), assuming outcome differences are negligible. However, the individual explicitly rejects this assumption, stating that the outcome difference is more important than the probability difference. The individual considers the outcomes as highly non-negligible, directly contradicting a core premise of the decision reason. Furthermore, while the decision reason would favor Lottery B (higher chance of 0 Euros), the individual’s reasoning leads toward preferring Lottery A due to the smaller worst-case loss, even though it has a lower probability of the favorable outcome. The information considered by the individual (magnitude of loss) and their justification (avoiding larger pain) do not align with the decision reason’s focus (probability of favorable outcome under negligible outcome differences). Therefore, the individual did not use this decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
